[Variation "Advance Variation, Short Variation"]
[ECO "B12"]
[Character "solid,positional"]
[Risk "low"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "none"]
[Themes "c5-break,good-bishop,attack-the-base"]
[For "black"]
[Kind "main"]
[Opponent "3.e5"]
[Start "short-system 4...e6"]

5. Be2 ( 5. Bd3 Bxd3 6. Qxd3 { The same position as 4.Bd3 Bxd3 5.Qxd3 e6 6.Nf3: Black plays ...c5, as in the Bd3 line. } ) ( 5. c3 c5 6. Be2 ( 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. Qxd3 { The Bd3 line again: Black plays ...Nc6 next. } ) ( 6. Bb5+ Nd7 { Blocks with the knight, which watches e5 and c5 from d7. Black can play ...a6 next to ask the bishop what it wants. } ) ( 6. Be3 Qb6 { Hits b2, which lost its guard when the bishop left c1, and adds a third attacker to d4. } ) 6... Nc6 7. O-O ) ( 5. Nc3 c5 6. Be2 ( 6. Bb5+ Nd7 7. O-O a6 8. Bxd7+ Qxd7 ) ( 6. Be3 c4 { The quiet way. After 6...cxd4 7.Nxd4 the knight chases the bishop on f5, and after 6...Nc6 White takes on c5 and keeps the pawn. Now the queenside is closed and Black plays ...Nc6 and ...b5. } ) ( 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. Qxd3 cxd4 { Opens the c-file and makes White spend a move taking back on d4. Black follows with ...Nc6 and ...Nge7. } ) ( 6. dxc5 Bxc5 ) ( 6. Bg5 Be7 { Blocks the attack on the queen and offers to trade White's active bishop. } ) 6... Nc6 7. O-O cxd4 { Swaps on d4 before White can support the pawn with c3. } 8. Nxd4 Nxd4 9. Qxd4 Ne7 { Heads for c6, where it hits the queen and keeps d4 under fire. } ) ( 5. c4 Ne7 { Develops and gets ready to recapture on d5 with the knight if White takes there. } ) ( 5. h3 c5 ) ( 5. Bg5 Qb6 { Steps out of the bishop's attack and hits b2, which lost its guard when the bishop left c1, and d4 as well. } ) 5... c5 { Attacks the base of White's pawn chain at d4. If that pawn goes, the pawn on e5 loses its support. } 6. Be3 ( 6. c3 { The most common move. It is the same position as 5.c3 c5 6.Be2: Black plays ...Nc6. } ) ( 6. dxc5 Bxc5 7. O-O Ne7 ) 6... cxd4 { Opens the c-file and gains time: White must recapture, and Black can then attack the piece that took. } 7. Nxd4 ( 7. Bxd4 Nc6 8. Bb5 ) 7... Ne7 { If White takes on f5, the knight recaptures. That keeps Black's pawns healthy, where ...exf5 would double them. } 8. Nd2 Nbc6 { Develops and attacks d4 again, so White must keep guarding it. } 9. N2f3 Bg4 { Attacks the knight on f3, which guards both d4 and e5. Trading it loosens White's grip on the centre. } 10. O-O Bxf3 { Takes the knight that guards e5 and d4. The bishop has done its job, and the e5 pawn loses a protector. } 11. Nxf3 Nf5 { Heads for d4 and hits the bishop on e3. If White leaves the book, trade pieces, castle after ...Be7, and keep aiming at d4 and e5. } *
